{%- comment -%}
  Catalogue card — ported from ProductCard.jsx. Matted product frame, display-face
  pricing, full-width CTA.

  React → Shopify mapping:
    p.onSale            → compare_at_price > price
    p.inStock           → product.available
    hasRange(p)         → product.price_varies
    p.type == variable  → more than one variant (→ "Choose options")
    bestseller          → product carries settings.bestseller_tag
    p.rating/p.reviews  → the standard reviews.rating / reviews.rating_count metafields
    sizeCount           → distinct Size values with a trailing "Hook"/"Loop" dropped,
                          matching the regex the React card used

  The whole card is clickable in the React version (onClick on the <article>);
  mts-theme.js reproduces that from data-mts-card-href so the markup and the CSS
  stay identical.

  Params: product (required)
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign on_sale = false
  if product.compare_at_price > product.price
    assign on_sale = true
  endif

  assign is_variable = false
  unless product.has_only_default_variant
    assign is_variable = true
  endunless

  assign bestseller = false
  if settings.bestseller_tag != blank and product.tags contains settings.bestseller_tag
    assign bestseller = true
  endif

  assign rating = product.metafields.reviews.rating.value.rating
  assign rating_count = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value

  # distinct sizes, ignoring the Hook/Loop suffix — "20mm x 25m Hook" and
  # "20mm x 25m Loop" are one size, which is what the card counts.
  assign size_terms = ''
  for opt in product.options_with_values
    assign opt_name = opt.name | downcase
    if opt_name contains 'size'
      for v in opt.values
        assign base = v | remove_last: ' Hook' | remove_last: ' Loop'
        assign size_terms = size_terms | append: base | append: '~'
      endfor
    endif
  endfor
  assign size_count = 0
  if size_terms != blank
    assign size_count = size_terms | split: '~' | uniq | size
  endif

  # The label used to be product.collections.first — whatever order Shopify
  # returned — so products advertised themselves as "Display Signage" or
  # "Building & Construction". Take the first collection that is in the CATEGORY
  # menu instead; anything else never becomes a label.
  assign card_cat = nil
-%}
{%- comment -%}
  The exclusion list is READ FROM THE INDUSTRIES MENU, not hardcoded.
  It used to be a literal list and had drifted four collections behind the menu
  — airconditioning-refrigeration-tapes, picture-framing-tapes,
  tapes-for-school-library and tapes-for-visual-arts-entertainment were all
  missing — so a duct tape happily labelled itself "Airconditioning
  Refrigeration Tapes". Reading the menu means adding an industry collection can
  never reintroduce that, and the literals below are only a floor in case the
  menu is renamed or absent.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign cat_handles = ',all,frontpage,bestsellers,' -%}
{%- for ind in linklists.industries.links -%}
  {%- if ind.url contains '/collections/' -%}
    {%- assign ind_handle = ind.url | split: '/collections/' | last | split: '?' | first | split: '#' | first -%}
    {%- assign cat_handles = cat_handles | append: ind_handle | append: ',' -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- for coll in product.collections -%}
  {%- if card_cat == nil -%}
    {%- assign needle = coll.handle | prepend: ',' | append: ',' -%}
    {%- unless cat_handles contains needle -%}
      {%- assign card_cat = coll -%}
    {%- endunless -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{%- comment -%}
  data-opt-* carries this product's option values for the derived Colour/Size
  filters (see mts-filter-panel). Values are pipe-delimited and lower-cased so a
  match is a simple substring test with delimiters, never a fuzzy one.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign opt_colour = ''
  assign opt_size = ''
  assign colour_values = blank
  for opt in product.options_with_values
    assign oname = opt.name | downcase
    if oname contains 'colour' or oname contains 'color'
      assign opt_colour = opt.values | uniq | join: '|' | downcase
      assign colour_values = opt.values | uniq
    elsif oname contains 'size'
      assign opt_size = opt.values | uniq | join: '|' | downcase
    endif
  endfor
-%}
<article class="pc" data-mts-card data-mts-card-href="{{ product.url | within: collection }}"
         data-opt-colour="|{{ opt_colour }}|" data-opt-size="|{{ opt_size }}|">
  <div class="pc__media">
    {%- if product.featured_media -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        PERFORMANCE: a collection page renders up to 48 of these. Serving one
        fixed 600px file to every card ships far more bytes than any card needs
        — the frame is ~300px on desktop and roughly half the viewport on
        mobile. srcset + sizes lets the browser pick the smallest file that
        still looks sharp, including on 2x screens.

        `index` is passed by the caller: the first row is what the user sees
        first, so it loads eagerly and everything below stays lazy.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign eager = false -%}
      {%- if index and index < 5 -%}{%- assign eager = true -%}{%- endif -%}
      <img
        src="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 400 }}"
        srcset="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 200 }} 200w,
                {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 300 }} 300w,
                {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w,
                {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w,
                {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w"
        sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 300px, (min-width: 960px) 24vw, (min-width: 640px) 32vw, 46vw"
        alt="{{ product.featured_media.alt | default: product.title | escape }}"
        width="300" height="300"
        decoding="async"
        {% if eager %}loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"{% else %}loading="lazy"{% endif %}>
    {%- else -%}
      {{ 'product-1' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'pc__placeholder' }}
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="pc__badges">
      {%- if bestseller -%}
        <span class="tag tag--best">{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'star', size: 11 %} {{ 'products.product.bestseller' | t }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if on_sale -%}
        <span class="tag tag--sale">{{ 'products.product.sale' | t }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Only the EXCEPTION gets a badge over the image. "In stock" is true of
        almost every line here, so as a filled green pill it shouted on every
        card, competed with the product photo and told the shopper nothing.
        It moved into the body as a quiet dot; out-of-stock stays a badge,
        because that one genuinely changes what you do next.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- unless product.available -%}
        <span class="tag tag--out">{{ 'products.product.out_of_stock_badge' | t }}</span>
      {%- endunless -%}
    </div>

    {%- if settings.show_wishlist -%}
      <button class="pc__wish" data-mts-wish="{{ product.handle }}"
              aria-label="{{ 'products.product.add_to_wishlist' | t }}" aria-pressed="false">
        {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'heart', size: 18 %}
      </button>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  <div class="pc__body">
    {%- comment -%}
      The category label is suppressed on a collection page. Standing on the
      Butyl Tape page and reading "BUTYL TAPE" above every single card tells the
      shopper nothing they don't already know — it is a whole extra line of
      shouting per card, and it pushes the product name down.

      It still shows everywhere the context ISN'T given: home page grids, search
      results, cart upsell and the wishlist, where "which kind of tape is this?"
      is a real question. `collection` is only set on collection templates, so
      it doubles as the test.

      DO NOT go back to testing the `collection` global here: {% render %} is
      scope-isolated and `collection` is NOT visible inside it, so the test was
      always true and the label never hid. The caller passes `hide_cat` instead.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if card_cat and card_cat.handle != 'all' and hide_cat != true -%}
      <span class="pc__cat">{{ card_cat.title }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}

    <h3 class="pc__name"><a href="{{ product.url | within: collection }}">{{ product.title }}</a></h3>

    <div class="pc__rating">
      {%- if rating -%}
        {% render 'mts-stars', rating: rating, count: rating_count, size: 14 %}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The "·" is a SEPARATOR between the star rating and the size count, but
        it was printed unconditionally — so on the many products with no reviews
        yet the card opened with a dangling "· 5 sizes". It now only appears
        when there is actually something to its left.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if size_count > 1 -%}
        <span class="pc__sizes num">{% if rating %}· {% endif %}{{ 'products.product.sizes_count' | t: count: size_count }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if product.available -%}
        <span class="pc__stock"><i class="pc__stock-dot"></i>{{ 'products.product.in_stock_badge' | t }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    <div class="pc__foot">
      <div class="pc__price">
        {%- if product.price_varies -%}
          <span class="pc__from">{{ 'products.product.from' | t }}</span> <b class="num">{{ product.price_min | money }}</b>
        {%- else -%}
          <b class="num">{{ product.price | money }}</b>
        {%- endif -%}
        <span class="pc__gst">{{ 'products.product.inc_gst' | t }}</span>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        COLOUR DOTS — the colours this product comes in, under the price.

        Values come from the Colour option, the same source the card already
        uses for its `data-opt-colour` filter payload, so a dot and the filter
        can never disagree about what colours exist.

        A value only earns a dot if mts-swatch-fill can resolve it to a real
        colour; unresolvable ones (e.g. "Assorted") are counted into the
        overflow rather than drawn as an empty box pretending to be a colour.

        Capped at 6 so a 14-colour product cannot push the CTA out of line and
        break the grid's even card heights.

        Decorative-but-informative: the row carries one aria-label naming every
        colour, and the individual dots are aria-hidden — otherwise a screen
        reader announces six unlabelled list items. Colour is never the ONLY
        carrier of this information; the value is in the label and on the PDP.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if colour_values != blank and colour_values.size > 0 -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign dots = ''
          assign shown = 0
          # `opt.values` yields ProductOptionValue DROPS, not strings, so
          # `| join` renders them as "[ProductOptionValueDrop]". Filters that
          # take a string (downcase, escape) coerce fine, which is why the dot
          # titles were right while the aria-label was garbage. Build the label
          # by appending each value through a filter instead of joining.
          # `append` coerces the drop to its name; `strip`/`join` do not — which
          # is exactly why the dot titles were correct while the label was not.
          assign colour_names = ''
          for v in colour_values
            unless forloop.first
              assign colour_names = colour_names | append: ', '
            endunless
            assign colour_names = colour_names | append: v
          endfor
          for v in colour_values
            capture fill
              render 'mts-swatch-fill', value: v
            endcapture
            assign fill = fill | strip
            if fill != blank and shown < 6
              assign dots = dots | append: fill | append: '~~' | append: v | append: '||'
              assign shown = shown | plus: 1
            endif
          endfor
          assign extra = colour_values.size | minus: shown
        -%}
        {%- if shown > 0 -%}
          <div class="pc__colours" aria-label="{{ 'products.product.available_colours' | t: colours: colour_names }}">
            {%- assign dot_list = dots | split: '||' -%}
            {%- for d in dot_list -%}
              {%- assign parts = d | split: '~~' -%}
              <span class="pc__dot" style="background:{{ parts[0] }}" title="{{ parts[1] | escape }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            {%- endfor -%}
            {%- if extra > 0 -%}
              <span class="pc__dot-more num" aria-hidden="true">+{{ extra }}</span>
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              The swatches say WHICH; this says HOW MANY. Without it a row of
              four discs is decoration — a shopper still has to count them to
              learn anything. It also gives the row a text baseline to align
              to, so it sits with the price instead of floating.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <span class="pc__colours-n">
              {{ 'products.product.colours_count' | t: count: colour_values.size }}
            </span>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if product.available -%}
        {%- if is_variable -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            Labelled "Add to cart" on request, but it stays an <a> to the
            product page rather than becoming a quick-add button.

            These products have real, price-changing options — 24mm x 50m and
            96mm x 50m of the same tape are different sizes at different
            prices. A card-level quick-add would have to pick a variant on the
            shopper's behalf, and picking the first one silently is how a
            customer ends up with the wrong width and a return. So the label
            reads as the intent and the click goes where the size is chosen.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <a class="pc__cta" href="{{ product.url | within: collection }}">
            {{ 'products.product.add_to_cart' | t }} {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'arrowRight', size: 15 %}
          </a>
        {%- else -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            Quick-add has to honour the product's minimum order quantity too.
            Adding 1 of a 6-minimum tape from a collection card would put an
            unsellable line in the cart that nothing downstream corrects.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- assign quick_min = product.metafields.mts.volume_tiers.value.min | default: 1 -%}
          <button class="pc__cta" type="button"
                  data-mts-quick-add="{{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id }}"
                  data-mts-quick-qty="{{ quick_min }}">
            {{ 'products.product.add_to_cart' | t }} {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'arrowRight', size: 15 %}
          </button>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- else -%}
        <button class="pc__cta pc__cta--out" type="button" disabled>{{ 'products.product.sold_out' | t }}</button>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
